 "result": "In this repo the class is `VerbView`, a subclass of `View`, both in `src/tessel/views.py`.\n\n**1. Collecting the allowed methods: `VerbView.__init_subclass__` (L165)**\n- When you subclass `VerbView`, this runs and builds `cls.methods` unless the class sets `methods` itself.\n- It starts from the parent classes' `methods`.\n- It then adds an uppercase entry for every name in `http_method_funcs` (L11) that the class defines, so `get()` and `post()` give `{\"GET\", \"POST\"}`.\n\n**2. Turning the class into a view function: `View.to_view_func(name, *args, **kwargs)` (L85)**\n- It's a classmethod that returns a plain `view(**kwargs)` function.\n- With `init_every_request=True` (the default), each request creates a new instance and calls `run_view`. With `False`, one shared instance is used.\n- It applies any `decorators` from the class, then attaches `view_class`, `__name__`, `methods` and `provide_automatic_options`.\n- Route registration (`attach_route_rule(..., view_func=MyView.to_view_func(\"name\"))`) can then read `methods` from the function.\n\n**3. Dispatching per request: `VerbView.run_view` (L182)**\n- It looks up `getattr(self, request.method.lower())`.\n- `HEAD` falls back to `get` if there is no `head` handler.\n- It asserts that a handler exists, then calls it through `active_app.ensure_sync`, so async handlers work too.\n\n`View.run_view` (L78) is the base version, which subclasses override and which raises `NotImplementedError`.",
